.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt SHUTDOWN 8
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm shutdown
.Nd halt or restart a served ABSD machine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Cm now
.Nm
.Fl h | Fl p | Fl r
.Cm now
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
asks the SSH server to end the boot in order: every session is told and
ends, the file server commits and stops, and the machine halts, or, with
.Fl r ,
restarts.
.Fl h
and
.Fl p
are the same as no option.
Any other argument is refused with status 2 and changes nothing.
.Pp
It is not a program, and the operator can ask in two ways:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql ssh host shutdown
The SSH server takes the command itself; nothing is started.
.It Ic shutdown No typed to an interactive shell
.Xr absd-sh 1
passes the words to the SSH server on the session's control channel and
prints the answer.
A shell that has no such channel (one started by another shell) answers
.Ql no session control channel .
.El
.Pp
Either way the SSH server decides: every authenticated session of the
operator may ask, and the first request wins; a later one is refused with
status 1
.Pq Ql a halt is already under way .
The server holds no right over the machine: it acts by ending its sessions
and exiting with a status that tells init which end was asked for.
Init holds the machine and ends the boot.
No session process holds any right to stop the machine.
.Pp
In order:
.Bl -enum
.It
The requesting session prints
.Ql ABSD is going down for halt: ...
and ends with status 0 (a shell prints it and then sees the end of its
input).
New connections are refused.
.It
Every session with something running is told
.Ql sshd: the system is going down for halt; this session ends
(on its terminal, or on standard error), and its input is closed after
what the client already sent.
A shell, or a command reading its input, finishes; the session ends with
its status as usual.
.It
After 10 seconds the server ends whatever still runs; that session closes
without an exit status (OpenSSH reports 255).
A session's shell is told first, on its control channel, and kills and
reaps the commands it is running before it exits.
The server kills a shell itself only if it has not exited within a second
of reading that, or has not read it within 10 seconds (a shell waiting on
the file server reads it late).
Commands cannot start programs
.Pq Xr absd-sh 1 ,
so a shell that ends its commands leaves nothing behind.
A shell the server had to kill
.Pq Ql SSH SESSION END ... forced=true
may leave the commands it was running; see
.Sx CAVEATS .
.It
The file server commits every session's files and stops.
Every write it acknowledged is on the disk.
.It
If a process the sessions started is still running, init waits up to 20
seconds for it to end; init has no right to end it.
With nothing left, init halts or restarts the machine.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when the request was accepted, 1 when another is already under way, 2
for a form this page does not list.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
On the serial console, in order:
.Ql SSH SHUTDOWN REQUESTED ,
.Ql SSH SHUTDOWN GRACE OVER
(only if something had to be ended),
.Ql SSH SESSION END ... forced=false
for each shell told to end,
.Ql SSH SHUTDOWN COMPLETE ,
.Ql FSD STOPPED ,
.Ql SERVED SHUTDOWN action=halt ... drained=true ,
and
.Ql ABSD BOOT OK .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr absd-sh 1 ,
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr filesystem 7 ,
.Xr handles 7 ,
.Xr afterboot 8 ,
.Xr reboot 8
.Pp
In the kernel repository:
.Pa docs/PROCESS-STARTUP.md
("Served boot and orderly shutdown").
.Sh QUALIFICATION
Qualified in a served boot
.Pq Ql net fsd served
under QEMU, where a halt ends QEMU with status 3.
A qualification boot
.Pq Ql net fsd
is ended by init's deadline for the SSH server instead, and
.Nm
there is not qualified; see
.Xr afterboot 8 .
On a physical machine a halt stops the CPU and leaves the machine powered
(there is no ACPI power-off); this is not tested.
.Sh CAVEATS
If a process is still running 20 seconds after the file server stopped,
init cannot end it.
A requested
.Nm
then stops the machine at once, after storage is committed (QEMU status
71,
.Ql SERVED SHUTDOWN UNREAPED ,
.Ql SERVED SHUTDOWN ESCALATED requested=halt reason=unreaped outcome=stop code=17 ) ;
a requested
.Nm reboot
records the same escalation
.Pq Ql outcome=recovery_reset
and takes the whole-machine recovery reset, so the machine comes back up
as asked, with the leftover process in the kernel's pre-reset census.
A file server that ends with a failure, or does not end within 30 seconds,
stops a requested halt with code 18 (QEMU status 75) and escalates a
requested reboot to the same recovery reset
.Pq Ql reason=file_server_status | file_server_timeout .
The escalation line is always printed before the stop or the reset; the
operator's request never silently becomes the other outcome.
That happens only if a shell was killed before it could end its commands
.Pq Ql SSH SESSION END ... forced=true
or the SSH server itself ended with sessions running.
A
.Ic reboot
with a command still running in a session restarts the machine when that
session's shell ends the command
.Pq Ql forced=false ,
the case the server is built for.
There is no delay form
.Pq Ql shutdown +5
and no message to the sessions beyond the notice.
.Pp
A failure of the SSH server or the file server during service (not a
requested end) resets the whole machine, at most three times in a row:
the file server counts the generations that reached service and did not
end as requested, on disk, before it serves
.Pq Ql FSD RECOVERY seq=N streak=K ... ,
and the fourth such failure stops the machine instead
.Pq Ql SERVED RECOVERY ESCALATED reason=recovery_budget_exhausted ... code=20 ,
QEMU status 83.
Only a requested
.Nm
or
.Nm reboot
that init completed as asked clears the count (the file server stopped
cleanly and every session process ended within the bound); an SSH server
that merely exits, or an end that escalates, does not.
After that stop the machine needs the console or a power cycle, and the
next boot serves once more with the count carried, so an operator can log
in and reboot it cleanly.
